# Rule: SwiftUI Application Architecture

Sources

https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/swiftui-concepts/exploring-the-structure-of-a-swiftui-app
https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/swiftui-concepts/specifying-the-view-hierarchy-of-a-scene
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/app-organization
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/scenes
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/windows
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/documents
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/documentgroup
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/building-a-document-based-app-using-swiftdata

Scope

Defines how SwiftUI applications are structured at the top level:
App -> Scenes -> Windows -> Root Views -> View Hierarchy

Intent

Ensure SwiftUI applications follow Apple's architectural model where:

The `App` type defines the application entry point
Scenes define major UI containers
Windows host view hierarchies
Root views initiate navigation and layout

This structure must remain predictable, modular, and scalable.

Core Architectural Principle

A SwiftUI app is defined as a hierarchy:

App
└─ Scene
└─ Window
└─ Root View
└─ View Hierarchy

Each level has a clearly defined responsibility.

The `App` coordinates application configuration.
`Scenes` represent independent UI environments.
`Windows` host root views.
Root views build the UI tree.

---

## APP ENTRY POINT

The application must define a single type conforming to `App`.

Responsibilities of the App:

define the root scene structure
configure global environment dependencies
configure persistence containers
register commands and menus
manage lifecycle events

Example

@main
struct InvoiceApp: App {

    var sharedModelContainer: ModelContainer = {
        try! ModelContainer(for: Client.self, Invoice.self)
    }()

    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            RootView()
        }
        .modelContainer(sharedModelContainer)
    }

}
Rules

- Only one @main App type should exist
- App must not contain UI logic
- App should configure shared dependencies
- App should define scene topology

SCENE ARCHITECTURE
Scenes represent independent UI environments.
Common SwiftUI scenes:

WindowGroup
Window
DocumentGroup
MenuBarExtra
Settings

Scene Responsibilities

provide a window environment
host root views
define lifecycle scope
manage scene-specific state

Example

var body: some Scene {

    WindowGroup("Invoices") {
        InvoiceWorkspaceView()
    }

    Settings {
        SettingsView()
    }

}
Rules

- Scenes must represent logical app workspaces
- Root views must be attached directly to scenes
- Scenes should not contain business logic
- Scene configuration must remain lightweight

WINDOW MANAGEMENT
Windows host SwiftUI view hierarchies.
Types of window containers:

WindowGroup
Window
DocumentGroup

WindowGroup

Creates multiple windows of the same content type.

Example

WindowGroup {
DashboardView()
}
Window
Creates a single uniquely identified window.

Example

Window("Inspector", id: "inspector") {
InspectorView()
}
Rules

- Use WindowGroup for primary workspace views
- Use Window for singleton tool windows
- Avoid complex logic inside window definitions
- Window content should be a root view

DOCUMENT-BASED APPLICATIONS
Document-based apps use DocumentGroup.
Responsibilities

manage file opening
manage file saving
integrate with system document browser

Example

DocumentGroup(newDocument: InvoiceDocument()) { file in
InvoiceEditor(document: file.$document)
}
Rules

- Document models must conform to FileDocument
- DocumentGroup must be defined in the App scene tree
- Document UI must be isolated to document editors

ROOT VIEW DESIGN
Each scene must host a single root view.
Root views typically:

establish navigation structure
provide environment objects
configure layout containers

Example

struct RootView: View {

    var body: some View {
        NavigationSplitView {
            SidebarView()
        } detail: {
            ContentView()
        }
    }

}
Rules

- Root views should define navigation containers
- Root views should not perform heavy data loading
- Root views should inject environment dependencies
- Root views must remain lightweight orchestration layers

ENVIRONMENT INJECTION
Global dependencies must be injected at the scene level.
Typical dependencies:

ModelContainer
services
actors
configuration objects

Example

WindowGroup {
RootView()
}
.modelContainer(sharedContainer)
.workspaceStandardServicesEnvironment(…)
// Workspace-scene `EnvironmentValues` keys (`\.geocodingService`, `\.swiftDataGeocodingService`, EventKit stores, NDIS hooks, template PDF protocols) and `workspaceStandardServicesEnvironment` live in the **WorkspaceUI** package (composition bridge: Core + Data + SharedUI). **SharedUI** does not import Data.

Rules

- Avoid global singletons
- Inject dependencies via environment
- Inject persistence containers at scene level

LIFECYCLE EVENTS
SwiftUI applications observe lifecycle changes via environment values.
Example

@Environment(\.scenePhase) var scenePhase

.onChange(of: scenePhase) { phase in
if phase == .background {
saveChanges()
}
}
Rules

- lifecycle logic belongs in App or Scene layers
- avoid scattering lifecycle handling throughout views

ARCHITECTURAL LAYERING
A well-structured SwiftUI application should follow this layering:
Application Layer
(App, Scenes)

UI Composition Layer
(Root views, navigation, layout)

Feature Layer
(feature view modules)

Domain Layer
(models, services)

Persistence Layer
(SwiftData, services, actors)

Example structure

App/
InvoiceApp.swift

Scenes/
MainWindowScene.swift

Features/
Clients/
Invoices/
Sessions/

Views/
Shared/

Models/
SwiftDataModels/

Services/

Persistence/
Rules

- UI views must not directly access persistence infrastructure
- domain logic must not exist inside views
- services should mediate between UI and persistence

ANTI-PATTERNS
Avoid the following architectural mistakes.
UI Logic in App

@main
struct App: App {
var body: some Scene {
WindowGroup {
if loggedIn {
Dashboard()
}
}
}
}
Correct approach
Use a root coordinator view.

Heavy Scene Configuration

Scenes performing data queries

Scenes must remain configuration layers only.

Multiple Root Views

WindowGroup {
SidebarView()
DetailView()
}
Correct
WindowGroup {
RootView()
}
CHECKLIST
Before implementing a SwiftUI application structure:

- Is there exactly one App entry point?
- Are scenes clearly defined and minimal?
- Does each scene host a single root view?
- Are global dependencies injected via environment?
- Is navigation defined in root views?
- Is UI separated from persistence logic?

EXPECTED ARCHITECTURE
Correct high-level architecture:
App
└─ Scene
└─ Window
└─ RootView
└─ Navigation Container
└─ Feature Views
